lazysite-nginx for Debian
=========================

nginx glue for lazysite (the SM139 glue packages): the shipped
server-block templates at /usr/share/lazysite-nginx/ and the
lazysite-nginx-vhost command that renders them into
/etc/nginx/sites-available/. It never touches site content, and it
prints - never runs - the enable/test/reload steps, so the host-wide
actions stay visible operator choices.

nginx has no CGI engine: both patterns run lazysite's CGI endpoints
through fcgiwrap (apt-get install fcgiwrap; Debian provides
/run/fcgiwrap.socket).

Two patterns (one vhost file each):

  vhost-cgi   Plain CGI via fcgiwrap. Static files are served directly;
              page misses go to the processor via the CGI auth wrapper
              (nginx has no FallbackResource - try_files + a named
              location is the equivalent). Every page render forks a
              CGI through fcgiwrap.
  vhost-fcgi  FastCGI pool - RECOMMENDED on nginx. Anonymous page
              traffic speaks native FastCGI to the persistent per-site
              lazysite@<domain> pool socket (~150x faster on cache
              hits, no fcgiwrap fork on the hot path); session-bearing
              page requests are carved out to the CGI auth wrapper
              (the pool is anonymous by design), and /manager,
              /cgi-bin, /dav stay on the fcgiwrap CGI path.

Which pattern: on nginx, prefer vhost-fcgi - the CGI pattern pays a
double fork (fcgiwrap + the CGI) on every page render and exists as the
no-daemon fallback. Switching is `lazysite-nginx-vhost add <domain>
--fcgi --force` plus the pool setup below - site content is untouched.

Per-domain wiring (3 steps)
---------------------------

1. Provision the site AS ITS USER (never root - see lazysite(1)):

       sudo -u siteuser lazysite provision \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html \
           --cgibin  /srv/www/example.com/cgi-bin \
           --domain  example.com

2. Render the vhost (as root; --cgibin defaults to the cgi-bin sibling
   of the docroot):

       lazysite-nginx-vhost add example.com --fcgi \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html            # pool
       lazysite-nginx-vhost add example.com \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html            # CGI

3. Run the printed steps: install fcgiwrap (once per host), link
   sites-enabled, nginx -t, reload. HTTPS:
   `certbot --nginx -d example.com`.

For --fcgi the pool must exist before pages render:
/etc/lazysite/pools/example.com.conf + `systemctl enable --now
lazysite@example.com` - the pattern is documented in the
lazysite-common README.Debian and in the unit file.

Note: the CGI runs as the fcgiwrap user (www-data on Debian) - the same
identity Apache's mod_cgi gives it, so the provisioned group/setgid
layout needs no adjustment.

`lazysite-nginx-vhost remove <domain>` deletes the vhost file only -
site content is never touched.

Trying lazysite without a web server: `lazysite demo` (lazysite-common)
provisions a scratch site and serves it on the built-in dev server.

The full multi-server reference (these patterns explained once, plus
Apache, Caddy, lighttpd and the generic front-end contract) is
/usr/share/doc/lazysite-nginx/webserver-wiring.md.

